𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “A Kubernetes worker node fails, and some application Pods become Pending. How will you troubleshoot and restore the workload?”

In production, I wouldn't immediately delete Pods or increase replicas. First, I'd understand why Kubernetes cannot schedule the affected workloads.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Identify Pending Pods

kubectl get pods -A -o wide
kubectl get nodes

Identify affected workloads and check whether the failed node is still available.

2️⃣ Inspect Pod events

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for "FailedScheduling", insufficient CPU/memory, taints, affinity rules or storage issues.

3️⃣ Check cluster capacity

kubectl top nodes
kubectl describe nodes

Verify whether the remaining nodes have enough allocatable resources to run the workloads.

4️⃣ Check scheduling constraints

Review:
🔹 CPU and memory requests
🔹 Node selectors and affinity
🔹 Taints and tolerations
🔹 PersistentVolumeClaims and volume topology

5️⃣ Check workload controllers

kubectl get deployment,rs -n <namespace>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

Confirm whether Deployments and ReplicaSets are maintaining the desired number of replicas.

6️⃣ Restore service safely

If capacity is insufficient, follow the approved recovery process to restore the failed node or add capacity. Verify that replacement Pods become Ready and the application is healthy.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

A Pending Pod does not always mean the application is broken. It may mean Kubernetes cannot find a suitable node to run it.
